# Code review

Pull requests are reviewed by **Macroscope** as well as by people. It reads for *intent*
rather than syntax, which makes it the one automated check that catches the class of
mistake generated code actually makes: the change that compiles, plans and passes every
linter while doing the wrong thing.

## Opening a PR is not the end of the task

The review takes a few minutes to arrive. A PR that is opened and immediately reported
as done is a PR whose review nobody read. Wait for it, read every comment, and act on it
before asking for a merge.

```bash
# Macroscope posts inline, as macroscopeapp[bot], on the review-comments endpoint.
gh api "/repos/<org>/<repo>/pulls/<N>/comments" \
  -q '.[] | select(.user.login=="macroscopeapp[bot]") | "\(.path):\(.line)\n\(.body)\n"'

# Nothing above usually means it has not run yet, not that it found nothing.
gh api "/repos/<org>/<repo>/pulls/<N>/reviews" -q '.[] | "\(.user.login) \(.state)"'
```

Poll rather than assume. A change spanning repositories gets a **separate review in
each**, so a clean PR in one says nothing about the others.

## Verify before acting

It is a model. Treat each comment as a lead, not a verdict:

- Check the claim against the upstream source **at the pinned version**, or against the
  live account — not against what an argument name suggests it does.
- Say what you found, including when the comment is wrong. That happens, and silently
  "fixing" a non-problem is worse than the comment was.
- A finding can be right about the mechanism and wrong about the impact. One correctly
  identified a security setting that was never actually being created; the resource still
  looked correct in the console, because a service default was doing the work. Real bug,
  different urgency — only checking the account separated the two halves.

## What it has caught in practice

A hardcoded database password. A security setting silently disabled by a flag whose name
suggested something else entirely. A retention policy that would have deleted existing
artifacts the first time a module was pointed at a real project. A data-source attribute
that does not exist.

None of these fail `tsc`, a linter, `tofu validate`, or `tofu plan`. That is the point.
